# CONTROL for do-block-on-inner-call.ex. Take the parentheses away and the
# defect goes away with them.
#
# `defp f do x end` is the same construct as `defp f(x) do x end` minus one
# thing: there is no inner CALL for the do-block to be hung on. `f` here is a
# bare identifier in `arguments`, not a call of its own, so when `do` arrives
# there is exactly one node that can take it and joints takes it.
#
# Both trees, leaf for leaf and extent for extent:
#
#   call [0, 15)
#     target: identifier  "defp"
#     arguments [5, 6)
#       identifier  "f"
#     do_block [7, 15)    <- sibling of arguments, on both sides
#
# This is what makes the guilty witness credible. The failure is not "joints
# cannot attach a do_block to a definition" - it attaches this one correctly.
# It is specifically the choice between two nodes that could both take it, and
# with only one candidate present there is no choice to get wrong.
roots 1
mends 0
holds do_block
spans do_block 7 15
spans arguments 5 6
